CORE
packed_array_part_select1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(##1 main\.a\[1\] == 171\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^\[main\.p1\] always \(##1 main\.a\[0\] == 205\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A non-indexed part-select on a packed array selects a range of
ELEMENTS, not bits (1800-2017 7.4.5/7.4.6, 11.5.1). For
reg [1:0][7:0] a, the write a[1:0] <= 16'hABCD must set element a[1] to
8'hAB and a[0] to 8'hCD.

ebmc instead treats the packed array as a flat vector: the type checker
(verilog_typecheck_exprt::convert_trinary_expr, the
ID_verilog_non_indexed_part_select case in
src/verilog/verilog_typecheck_expr.cpp) calls require_vector(src) and
types a[1:0] as unsignedbv{msb-lsb+1} = 2 bits, interpreting the indices
as bit indices. verilog_rtl_buildert::decompose_lhs
(src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp) then writes only a 2-bit slice
(--show-rtl shows "main.a[1:0] register, next-state value: 2'b01"), so
both properties are wrongly REFUTED. Found while auditing
verilog_rtl.cpp; the root cause is in the type checker.
